<BaseLayout
  title="Preparing Text Before Creating a PDF — Paul Digital Guides"
  description="Use these simple checks to organize text and make a document easier to read before creating or sharing a PDF."
  type="article"
>
  <article>
    <header class="page-hero">
      <div class="container">
        <span class="eyebrow">Paul Digital guide</span>
        <h1>Preparing text before creating a PDF</h1>
        <p>A few simple checks can make a text document easier to read and share.</p>
        <p class="guide-meta" style="margin-top: 1rem">
          <time datetime="2026-10-03">October 3, 2026</time> · 3 min read
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>
    <div class="content-section">
      <div class="container prose">
        <p>Taking a moment to organize text before turning it into a document can make the result easier to review. These general checks apply regardless of which document tool you use.</p>

        <h2>1. Put the content in a clear order</h2>
        <p>Arrange the title, headings and paragraphs in the order you want someone to read them. Keep related points together and remove duplicate lines.</p>

        <h2>2. Check spacing and line breaks</h2>
        <p>Look for accidental blank lines, split words or headings separated from the text that follows. A quick read-through often catches these issues.</p>

        <h2>3. Use simple, consistent formatting</h2>
        <p>Consistent headings and paragraph breaks are easier to scan than a mixture of spacing styles. Keep any symbols or special characters that matter to the meaning.</p>

        <h2>4. Review the finished document</h2>
        <p>After creating a PDF, open it and check that the content is complete, readable and arranged as expected before sharing it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
